+\subsection{Core Experiment}
+The experiment is deliberately simple. We build one synthetic, group-locked public-proxy corpus; train and calibrate accepted human-engineered detector branches and a prior layered fusion baseline; discover and lock an EI sparse calibrated fusion candidate using train/CV rows only; and score the blind holdout once. The reader-facing question is whether EI improves the low-FPR operating KPI against accepted best practice without using holdout tuning or forbidden generator metadata.
+
 \begin{itemize}
-  \item a signal-chain model card with nominal bands, bandwidths, PRF/CPI, pulse counts, range bins, and resolution assumptions;
-  \item scenario-balance and leakage diagnostics that cover split $\times$ site/range/aspect/noise/hard-negative family counts, marginal imbalance scores, a stratum-only baseline, a metadata-only baseline, a label-shuffle sanity test, and a nearest-neighbor train/holdout scan;
-  \item holdout metrics with counts, fixed-FPR recall, calibration, Brier score, ECE, and group-block bootstrap confidence intervals;
-  \item engineered-intelligence transparency for the advanced fusion candidate, with reader-facing modality and ablation summaries and locked evidence artifacts in the appendix bundle;
-  \item public-source detector archetype cards and a regional bird/RC hard-negative taxonomy for low-FPR false-alarm analysis;
-  \item a compare-only KTH anchor overlay for hard-negative realism and observable-shape checks.
+  \item \textbf{Corpus and split:} 10,000 scenario groups, 30,000 phase records, group-locked train/CV and holdout assignment.
+  \item \textbf{Accepted best-practice comparator:} classical radar/cue branches plus human-engineered prior layered fusion.
+  \item \textbf{EI candidate:} train/CV-only score search, sparse nonnegative late fusion, geodesic-odds calibration, and a written selection lock.
+  \item \textbf{Main outcome:} LCB95 Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR improves from 0.083 to 0.699; selected-threshold false positives fall from 49 to 1.
+  \item \textbf{Boundary:} all results are \proxyBoundary{}.
 \end{itemize}
 
